# XSH fdatasync()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# fdatasync() is the Synchronized Input and Output option (SIO). Whether
# data reached a storage device cannot be observed without a crash and is
# not tested; the assertions check return values, errors, and that the call
# does not disturb the file. Error tests arrange exactly one error
# condition (XSH 2.3).

assertion  XSH.fdatasync.001
anchor     functions/fdatasync.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fdatasync.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       SIO
since      2001
says       fdatasync() of a descriptor open for writing on a regular file
           that was just written returns 0, and afterwards the file has
           the same length and contents and the descriptor the same file
           offset.
note       NOTINUSE where fpathconf(_PC_SYNC_IO) says synchronized I/O
           is not supported for the file: EINVAL is then allowed.
test       t/001-regular.c

assertion  XSH.fdatasync.002
anchor     functions/fdatasync.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      conditional
when       SIO
since      2001
says       fdatasync() of a descriptor that is not open, and of -1, fails
           with EBADF.
test       t/002-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.fdatasync.003
anchor     functions/fdatasync.html#APPLICATION USAGE
anchor     functions/fdatasync.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       SIO
since      2001
says       fdatasync() of a descriptor open only for reading on a regular
           file that another descriptor has written returns 0.
note       NOTINUSE as XSH.fdatasync.001.
test       t/003-rdonly.c

assertion  XSH.fdatasync.004
anchor     functions/fdatasync.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      disputed
when       SIO
since      2001
says       fdatasync() of the read end of a pipe either returns 0 or
           fails with EINVAL; any other outcome is a failure.
note       disputed (new: fsync-pipe): EINVAL is required where the
           implementation does not support synchronized I/O for the
           file, and nothing says which file types those are; both
           outcomes are accepted. See XSH.fsync.003.
test       t/004-pipe.c

assertion  XSH.fdatasync.005
anchor     functions/fdatasync.html#APPLICATION USAGE
anchor     functions/fdatasync.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      conditional
when       SIO
since      2001
says       fdatasync() of a descriptor open on a directory either returns
           0 or fails with EINVAL; any other outcome is a failure.
note       The usage section says an application can call it on a
           directory; a file system that does not support synchronized
           I/O for it may report EINVAL.
test       t/005-directory.c
